{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Conviction-based eviction causes under the Eviction Law","requirement":"A landlord may evict a tenant convicted of, or who pleaded guilty to, assault, theft, drug activity or human trafficking, or who knowingly permits such a person to reside there. Three days' Notice to Quit required; suit within two years.","key_value":"3-day Notice to Quit; suit within 2 years","coverage_conditions":"Residential tenants of leased premises, and tenants who permit a person they know was convicted of these offenses to reside on the premises; covers offenses on the premises.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"residential tenancies; offenses committed on or upon the leased premises"},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.1","quoted_span":"No eviction suit may be brought more than two years after: the juvenile was found to be delinquent; conviction of the person; or after the person’s release from incarceration, whichever is later. A Notice to Quit must be served on the tenant at least three day s prior to filing a suit for (N.J.S.A. 2A-18-61.1(o)).","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Owner-occupied two- and three-family dwelling eviction causes and notice","requirement":"A tenant in an owner-occupied two- or three-family dwelling may be evicted only for holding over, nonpayment, disorderly conduct, willful damage, violation of written rules or lease re-entry terms: three-month notice for at-will or year-to-year tenancies, one month for month-to-month, three days for cause.","key_value":"3-month notice (at-will/year-to-year); 1 month (month-to-month); 3-day Notice to Quit for cause","coverage_conditions":"Tenants of landlord-occupied two- and three-family dwellings; removal only by court order.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":2,"max_units":3,"owner_dependent":true,"other":"landlord-occupied two- and three-family dwellings"},"exemptions":"No further notice is required before bringing action in court where the tenant stays after a failure to pay rent.","penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.2","quoted_span":"A three (3) month notice to quit must be gi ven if an at will tenancy or year -to-year tenancy exists. A one (1) month notice to quit is required for a month-to-month tenancy and other types of tenancies are entitled to a one (1) month notice to quit.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Rooming and boarding house evictions must follow good cause and notice requirements","requirement":"Owners of rooming and boarding houses must follow the good causes and notice requirements of the Eviction Law when evicting residents, and give at least three working days' notice to the County Welfare Board before starting an eviction action, unless the Bureau orders otherwise.","key_value":"3 working days' notice to County Welfare Board","coverage_conditions":"Buildings with at least two living units occupied by unrelated persons without private kitchens and bathrooms; exceptions include hotels over 85 percent temporary occupancy, dormitories and certain residences for the disabled.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":2,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"rooming and boarding houses"},"exemptions":"Hotels with more than 85 percent temporary occupancy by people with homes elsewhere, school and college dormitories, buildings housing only college students and certain residences for the disabled.","penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.A.C. 5:27-3.3(c)","quoted_span":"The Regulations Governing Rooming and Boarding Houses, which are enforced by the Bureau of Rooming and Boarding House Standards of the Department of Community Affairs, require owners of rooming and boarding houses to follow the good causes and notice requirements of the Eviction Law when evicting residents, except if otherwise ordered by the Bureau (N.J.A.C. 5:27-3.3(c)).","interaction":null,"confidence":0.75,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Penalties for eviction law violations (owner occupancy and removal from residential use)","requirement":"Landlord who gave notice to personally occupy must occupy at least six months; otherwise treble damages plus attorney fees. Landlord who retired a building from residential use may not use it residentially for five years, or face treble damages and up to $10,000 per violation.","key_value":"6 months' owner occupancy; 5-year residential use ban; treble damages; $10,000 civil penalty","coverage_conditions":"Landlords who evicted tenants after giving notice of personal occupancy of the unit or of permanently boarding up, demolishing or retiring the building from residential use.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"landlords who gave owner-occupancy or permanent-removal notices"},"exemptions":null,"penalty":"Three times the damages suffered plus attorney fees and costs; civil penalty up to $10,000 for each violation; property may not be registered as a planned real estate development during the five-year period.","effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.6","quoted_span":"If instead the landlord permits personal occupancy of the unit by another tenant or registration of conversion of the property to a condominium or cooperative, the landlord is liable to the former tenant for three (3) times the damages suffered plus attorney fees and costs (N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.6(a)).","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Reprisal - civil rights of tenants","requirement":"A landlord may not retaliate by eviction, lease alteration or refusal to renew because a tenant enforces lease or State or local law rights, makes a good faith complaint to a government authority after written notice to the landlord, or joins a tenant organization.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"Applies to all rental properties used for dwelling purposes, including mobile homes, except owner-occupied two- or three-family dwellings.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":true,"other":"all rental properties used for dwelling purposes including mobile homes; owner-occupied two- and three-family dwellings excepted"},"exemptions":"Owner-occupied two- or three-family dwellings.","penalty":"The tenant may sue the landlord for damages in a civil action.","effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:42-10.10","quoted_span":"A landlord cannot take reprisal action against a tenant by eviction, substantial alteration of a lease or its terms, or refusal to renew a lease when a tenant exercises certain civil rights (N.J.S.A. 2A:42-10.10).","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Foreclosure eviction notice (90-day notice to quit)","requirement":"Foreclosure alone is not grounds for eviction. A buyer who wants to occupy must give a 90-day notice to quit, and a lease running beyond 90 days must expire; new owners must notify tenants within 10 business days of the sale.","key_value":"90-day notice to quit","coverage_conditions":"Residential tenants in foreclosed properties; month-to-month tenants and two- and three-family owner-occupied units are not exempt from the 90-day notice.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"residential tenants in foreclosed properties"},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:50-69 et seq.","quoted_span":"Before a tenant can be evicted due to foreclosure, the landlord must provide the tenant with a 90 -day notice to qui t when the foreclosed property has been purchased by a buyer who wants to personally occupy it as his or her primary residence.","interaction":"Federal law does not preempt State or local law that provides longer time periods or other additional protections for tenants in foreclosure proceedings.","confidence":0.75,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Senior Citizens and Disabled Protected Tenancy Act","requirement":"Tenants who are at least 62, permanently disabled, or certain disabled veterans may be protected from eviction for 40 years if their family income is not more than three times the county average per-person income or $50,000, whichever is greater.","key_value":"40 years' protection; income at or below 3x county average per-person income or $50,000, whichever is greater","coverage_conditions":"Tenants at least 62 before the conversion recording date, permanently disabled, or honorably discharged and 60 percent or more service-disabled, who lived in the building being converted to condominium, cooperative or fee simple ownership at least one year before the conversion recording date.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"buildings converted to condominium, cooperative or fee simple ownership"},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.22 to -61.39","quoted_span":"Tenants may be protected from eviction for 40 years if their family income is not more than three (3) times the average per person income in their county or $50,000.00, whichever is greater (N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.28).","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Tenant Protection Act of 1992 (Hudson County protected tenancy)","requirement":"The Tenant Protection Law of 1992 extends protected tenancy to qualified tenants in qualified counties - currently only Hudson County - who were not eligible as senior citizens or disabled persons under the 1981 Act.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"Qualified tenants in qualified counties (currently only Hudson County) in buildings converted or being converted who were not eligible under the Senior Citizens and Disabled Protected Tenancy Act of 1981.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"converted or converting buildings in qualified counties, currently Hudson County only"},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.40 to -61.59","quoted_span":"The Tenant Protection Law of 1992 amendment extends protections to qualified tenants in qualified counties in buildings converted or being converted who were not eligible for Protected Tenancy as either Senior Citizens or Disabled Persons under the “Senior Citizens and Disabled Protected Tenancy Act of 1981” (N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.40 to -61.59; N.J.A.C. 5:24-3.1 to -3.4).","interaction":null,"confidence":0.75,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
